{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>From the choicest of the heavens</b> — megad is a term meaning delicacies and sweetness. And <b>from the choicest of the sun's yield</b> — that his land was open to the sun, which sweetens the fruits. <b>The produce of the moons</b> — there are fruits that the moon ripens, and they are cucumbers and gourds. <b>Géresh yaréachim</b> — the earth brings forth and produces from month to month; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:14). If so, the expression <b>peri megadim</b> (Song of Songs 4:13) would mean fruits of sweetness. He blesses him that his land will be from the sweetness of the heavens, meaning from the dew, and that it will be blessed from <b>tehom</b>, that the deep waters will lie beneath his land, so that springs and deep waters emerge in the valley and on the mountain. And it is also possible that the fruits are called <b>megadim</b>, and he says that his land will be blessed by the fruit of the heavens, which is the dew, and since these delicacies are in the dew they are called <b>megad</b>; and it will be blessed by <b>tehom</b> beneath it — and his blessing is like that of Jacob, who said (Genesis 49:25), \"blessings of the heavens above, blessings of <b>tehom</b> couching beneath.\" And the meaning of <b>géresh yaréachim</b> — the delicacies that the months bring forth, for all the trees in their months will sprout and blossom to put forth a branch and make fruit; and this is the meaning of <b>laḥodashav yevakker</b> (Ezekiel 47:12) — in its months known for that fruit, it yields first, before all the other trees."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:57.189856922Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}